const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is systemic sclerosis?',
    a: 'Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is a serious autoimmune disease in which the immune system, blood vessels and connective tissue all go wrong together — leading to hardening and scarring of the skin and, often, internal organs such as the lungs, gut and heart. The diffuse cutaneous form progresses faster and reaches deeper into the organs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does systemic sclerosis have to do with melanocortins?',
    a: 'MC1R sits not only on pigment cells but on immune cells, vascular endothelium and fibroblasts — the three cell types that drive sclerosis. Activating MC1R in preclinical models damps inflammation, eases vascular dysfunction and suppresses fibrosis, which is why an oral MC1R agonist is being tested as a potential disease-modifying therapy.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a melanocortin drug for systemic sclerosis?',
    a: 'Dersimelagon (MT-7117), an oral selective MC1R agonist best known for its Phase 3 programme in erythropoietic protoporphyria, also has a Phase 2 proof-of-concept study in diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis (73 patients). Nothing melanocortin-based is approved for the disease.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as using melanocortins for pigment?',
    a: 'Same receptor, different job. In EPP, MC1R activation builds protective eumelanin. In systemic sclerosis the bet is anti-inflammatory and anti-fibrotic signalling on immune, vascular and fibroblast cells — pigment side effects can still appear because the receptor is shared.',
  },
];

