<PageLayout
  title="Systemic sclerosis: pointing the pigment receptor at fibrosis"
  seoTitle="Systemic sclerosis & MC1R pharmacology"
  kicker="The condition"
  description="Systemic sclerosis scars skin and organs via immune, vascular and fibroblast failure. MC1R sits on all three — why an oral MC1R agonist is tested as more than a pigment drug."
  thread="A receptor famous for colour, asked to quiet the scarring that hardens skin and lungs."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Systemic scleroderma', type: 'MedicalCondition', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systemic_scleroderma'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin 1 receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_1_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Dersimelagon', type: 'Drug', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dersimelagon'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      'Systemic sclerosis is an autoimmune disease of immune activation, vasculopathy and progressive fibrosis of skin and internal organs — especially in the diffuse cutaneous form.',
      'MC1R is expressed on immune cells, vascular endothelium and fibroblasts, not only on melanocytes — so the pigment receptor is also a plausible anti-inflammatory and anti-fibrotic lever.',
      'In preclinical models, the oral MC1R agonist dersimelagon showed disease-modifying effects across inflammation, vascular dysfunction and fibrosis.',
      'A Phase 2 proof-of-concept study enrolled 73 patients with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis; no melanocortin drug is approved for the disease.',
      'This is MC1R’s second life beyond pigment — the same receptor dersimelagon uses for EPP, pointed at scarring.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Most of this site meets <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> as the pigment switch. Systemic
    sclerosis is the case that forces a wider reading: the same receptor sits on the cells that
    drive a fibrotic autoimmune disease, and turning it on looks, in models, like a way to calm
    inflammation <em>and</em> slow scarring.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>What breaks</h2>
  <p>
    Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is not one lesion. It is a triad — autoimmunity,
    microvascular injury, and progressive deposition of collagen — that hardens the skin and
    often reaches the lungs, gut, heart and kidneys. The <strong>diffuse cutaneous</strong> form
    spreads faster and deeper. Disease-modifying options remain limited relative to the damage
    the disease can do, which is why any pathway that touches all three arms of the triad draws
    attention.
  </p>

  <h2>Why MC1R is on the board</h2>
  <p>
    MC1R is best known on melanocytes, but it is also expressed on macrophages and other immune
    cells, on the endothelium that lines blood vessels, and on fibroblasts — exactly the cast
    that drives sclerosis. Preclinical work with{' '}
    <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a> (MT-7117), an oral selective MC1R agonist, reported
    disease-modifying effects in models of systemic sclerosis: less inflammatory signalling,
    improved vascular function, and suppressed fibrosis.<Cite n={1} /> That is the melanocortin
    anti-inflammatory axis from the <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page, extended into
    connective-tissue scarring.
  </p>

  <h2>What the pipeline is doing</h2>
  <p>
    Dersimelagon’s lead programme is in <a href="/epp">erythropoietic protoporphyria</a>, where
    its Phase 3 INSPIRE trial met its primary sunlight-tolerance endpoint in 2026.<Cite n={4} />
    Its second life is immunology: a Phase 2 proof-of-concept study enrolled{' '}
    <strong>73</strong> patients with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis to test whether the
    same once-daily oral MC1R agonist can modify disease in people.<Cite n={2} /> The molecule’s
    human pharmacology — and its expected pigment side effects of hyperpigmentation and
    lentigines — are already characterised from earlier studies.<Cite n={3} /> On the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> table this appears as the separate “Dersimelagon
    (immunology)” row, still early relative to the EPP programme.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Systemic sclerosis is a high-stakes, still-unproven test of whether MC1R activation can be
    disease-modifying outside pigment biology. The preclinical rationale is coherent; the
    clinical answer is not in yet. For the drug see <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a>; for
    neighbouring resolution programmes see <a href="/resomelagon">resomelagon</a>,{' '}
    <a href="/ulcerative-colitis">ulcerative colitis</a>, and{' '}
    <a href="/membranous-nephropathy">membranous nephropathy</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--accent-soft)] p-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page explains a disease and an investigational approach. It does not provide dosing
      or usage instructions and is not medical advice. Dersimelagon is not approved for systemic
      sclerosis. See the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <FAQ items={faqs} />

  <References items={references} />

  <RelatedPages slug="systemic-sclerosis" />
</PageLayout>
